Background
Stiff-person syndrome (SPS), also known as stiff-man
syndrome, was first reported in 1956. 1 It is an uncommon
neurologic disorder characterized by muscle stiffness,
rigidity, and muscle spasms, and it increases the patient ’s
risk of falls. Stiff-person syndrome occurs in women 2 to 3
times more frequently than men, and the prevalence is
approximately 1 in 1 million.
2,3 Most patients with SPS
present with autoantibodies against glutamic acid decar-
boxylase. This can lead to inhibition of glutamic acid
decarboxylase and gamma-amino butyric acid (GABA)
